# Hotel Playwright Architecture

**Status:** Architecture proposal / inspection record  
**Scope:** Playwright as a background collection engine for permitted hotel price and availability observations.  
**Important:** This document describes the proposed design. No Playwright scraper, job worker, source adapter, migration, or API change is implemented yet.

## 1. Current architecture

The project is an existing Django 6 / Django REST Framework application with a Vite React frontend. The current hotel implementation is additive to the existing datasets and navigation architecture and must remain backward compatible.

### Backend

- `backend/hotels/models.py`
  - `Hotel` is the canonical normalized accommodation record.
  - `HotelPriceObservation` stores observed room prices tied to dates, occupancy, currency, and source.
  - Missing values use `null`, empty strings, or `None`; unknown price is not represented as zero.
  - `Hotel` has a conditional uniqueness constraint on `(source, external_id)` and indexes for name, geography, and source identity.
  - `HotelPriceObservation` has a uniqueness constraint across hotel name, room type, dates, occupancy, currency, and source.
- `backend/hotels/views.py`
  - Authenticated list and viewport queries.
  - `POST /api/hotels/search-area/` optionally refreshes OSM/Overpass data and returns local matches.
  - Discovery, nearby search, geocoding, price CSV validation/import/comparison, and source transparency endpoints.
  - Viewport and search-area responses aggregate the lowest observed price for a hotel and calculate straight-line distance.
- `backend/hotels/urls.py` exposes the existing endpoints; no new search/status/job endpoints exist yet.
- `backend/data_sources/`
  - `client.py` provides an allowlisted, SSRF-checked HTTP client, timeouts, cache, bounded retries, and backoff.
  - `geocoding.py` provides explicit, user-driven Nominatim geocoding, Nepal-only filtering, a 1.1-second request interval, and cache.
  - `overpass.py` provides bounded OSM geographic discovery and caching.
  - `normalizers.py` normalizes OSM records while preserving provenance and null values.
  - `deduplication.py` conservatively matches same-source IDs, or normalized name plus close geography/address. It never merges only on a similar name.
  - `pricing.py` validates positive prices, dates, occupancy, currency, taxes, fees, and source metadata. It deliberately leaves availability `None` unless explicitly supplied by a permitted source.
- `backend/config/settings.py`
  - Uses `python-decouple` for environment configuration.
  - Uses Django’s local-memory cache by default.
  - Uses JWT authentication and Django/DRF permissions.
  - Has no Playwright, Celery, Django-Q, Huey, RQ, Redis, or other background worker dependency.
- `backend/requirements.txt` does not currently include Playwright or a job queue.
- Hotel tests use mocked external HTTP calls and cover the current API/data-quality behavior; no live third-party scraping tests are part of normal CI.

### Frontend

- `frontend/src/pages/Hotels.jsx` is a protected route with Search Hotels, Hotel Explorer, Nearby, Price Comparison, and Data Sources tabs. Search Hotels is the current default.
- `frontend/src/components/hotels/HotelSearchTab.jsx`
  - Explicit destination geocoding on submit/Enter; no keystroke autocomplete.
  - Internal destination coordinates are used only to recenter the map.
  - Search-area loading uses the current viewport and stale-boundary detection.
  - Responsive list/map toggle and desktop split layout.
  - Current filter/sort state is sent to existing viewport/search-area APIs.
- `frontend/src/components/hotels/HotelMap.jsx`
  - Leaflet with OpenStreetMap tiles and attribution.
  - Pan/zoom, marker selection, card/marker synchronization, price markers when a real observed price exists, lightweight grid clustering, recenter-by-coordinate, and Search This Area.
  - It currently does not expose a user-facing empty-map-click “Search hotels here” action or a separate visible recenter button.
- `frontend/src/components/hotels/HotelSearchResults.jsx`
  - Accessible result cards with name, location, stars, distance, observed price, source, and source link.
  - Technical provenance is currently shown in the card and will be moved/condensed as the UX evolves.
- `frontend/src/services/hotelAPI.js` calls the existing authenticated hotel endpoints and does not yet call a search-status or job-status endpoint.
- `frontend/package.json` contains React, Leaflet, React Leaflet, and Vitest/testing-library tooling. It does not contain a Playwright frontend package or a browser-clustering dependency.

## 2. Architectural decision

Playwright will be a **background, permitted-source observation collector**, not a synchronous dependency of the hotel search request.

The normal user search flow remains:

```text
User submits destination/dates/guests
  -> Django validates and geocodes
  -> database/local cache results are returned immediately
  -> freshness is evaluated
  -> if stale and enabled, a background refresh is scheduled
  -> worker runs configured source adapters
  -> normalized/validated/deduplicated observations are persisted
  -> search cache is invalidated
  -> frontend can poll status or refresh when new observations exist
```

The browser must never be launched from a normal request merely to populate the initial result list. A search request may return stale observations and a refresh state; it must not wait for page navigation or multiple third-party sites.

## 3. Proposed subsystem

The preferred location is below the existing data-source package, with no duplicate hotel domain model:

```text
backend/data_sources/playwright/
    __init__.py
    browser.py       # browser process/context/page lifecycle
    manager.py       # bounded source orchestration and job execution
    base.py          # adapter contract and search types
    models.py        # scraper job and durable job state
    normalizer.py    # source-neutral observation records
    validators.py    # data quality and condition checks
    exceptions.py    # blocked, timeout, navigation, parse, etc.
    adapters/
        __init__.py
        <configured-source-adapter>.py
    fixtures/        # deterministic HTML/JSON fixtures
    tests/           # mocked browser/adapter/parser tests
```

This is a proposal. It should be adapted to the project’s actual migration and app conventions before implementation.

### 3.1 Browser manager responsibilities

`browser.py` should:

- Lazily start one Chromium `Browser` process per worker process.
- Reuse the process across jobs; create an isolated `BrowserContext` per source/job.
- Create a new page for each search task and close it in `finally`.
- Close contexts and the browser during worker shutdown.
- Recreate a crashed browser once at the manager boundary, with bounded recovery.
- Enforce configurable navigation and action timeouts.
- Enforce configured browser type (`chromium` initially), headless/headed mode, concurrency, and optional legitimate proxy settings.
- Reject navigation outside the adapter’s configured source-domain allowlist before opening a page.
- Never accept a user-supplied arbitrary URL as a scrape target.

The manager should be process-local and safe for a single worker. If multiple workers are introduced later, browser processes should not be shared across processes.

### 3.2 Adapter contract

A source adapter should implement a small async contract conceptually equivalent to:

```python
class HotelSourceAdapter:
    name = "configured_source"
    display_name = "Configured Source"
    allowed_hosts = frozenset({"example.com"})
    enabled = False
    rate_limit_seconds = 30
    timeout_ms = 30000
    freshness_seconds = 1800

    async def search(
        self, destination, check_in, check_out, guests, rooms=1, filters=None
    ) -> list[dict]:
        raise NotImplementedError
```

The adapter owns source-specific interaction and extraction. It may use semantic roles, accessible labels, stable attributes, structured data, and page responses that are part of normal page operation. It must not call private/internal endpoints when doing so violates the source’s access rules.

The generic manager should not know selectors. The generic normalizer should not know source-specific DOM details. The adapter returns raw/source-specific records plus provenance and search conditions; normalization and validation happen after collection.

### 3.3 Registry and feature flags

A configuration-driven registry should map source keys to adapter classes and settings:

```text
HOTEL_SOURCES = {
    "source_a": {
        "adapter": SourceAAdapter,
        "enabled": ...,
        "display_name": ...,
        "rate_limit": ...,
        "timeout": ...,
        "freshness": ...,
        "supported_fields": ...,
    },
}
```

A source is queried only when it is enabled, its adapter is configured, and the request’s source policy permits it. Disabled or unconfigured sources are never visited. A first source adapter must be selected only after confirming the site is publicly accessible and automation is permitted; no source URL or live result should be claimed in this document.

## 4. Data flow and persistence

```text
Search conditions
  -> Playwright source adapter
  -> raw source records
  -> source-neutral normalizer
  -> validator (VALID / WARNING / INVALID)
  -> existing conservative hotel deduplication
  -> existing Hotel match/create
  -> HotelPriceObservation upsert
  -> cache invalidation
  -> job completion/status
  -> frontend refresh
```

### 4.1 Hotel matching

Playwright results should match existing `Hotel` records using the existing conservative policy:

1. Stable source identifier and source identity.
2. Normalized source URL where it is a reliable stable identity.
3. Normalized name plus close coordinates.
4. Normalized name plus substantial address match.

Name similarity alone is insufficient. Preserve source-specific hotel identities when a match is uncertain.

### 4.2 Price observation conditions

The existing `HotelPriceObservation` already stores hotel, name, room type, dates, occupancy, currency, base/taxes/fees/total, availability, source, source URL, and retrieval time. It is the first persistence target.

A price observation must not be saved unless its price and required conditions are valid. Missing values remain `None`. The scraper must not convert “unknown availability” to false, convert “unknown price” to zero, or infer tax inclusion.

If the required comparison conditions cannot be represented in the current schema, add narrowly scoped nullable fields in a later migration, for example:

- `rooms`
- `bed_type`
- `cancellation_policy`
- `breakfast_included`
- `payment_condition`
- `rating`
- `review_count`
- `search_conditions` (JSON)
- `raw_source_key` or a constrained source metadata identifier if needed

Do not add a second hotel model. Avoid over-indexing. Candidate indexes should be justified by actual search queries, especially hotel/date/source and retrieval time.

### 4.3 Provenance

Every saved observation must preserve:

- source key/display name
- source URL
- retrieval timestamp
- destination/dates/guests/rooms
- adapter version or source configuration version if practical
- validation status/warnings

The existing Data Sources area can expose this metadata. Raw HTML or raw payloads should be optional, size-limited, retention-controlled, and must not contain credentials, cookies, tokens, payment data, or unnecessary personal information. Normalized records should be the default long-term storage.

## 5. Background jobs and freshness

The repository has no existing queue or worker infrastructure. The smallest maintainable initial implementation should therefore be a database-backed job model plus a separate worker process/management command, not a synchronous request and not an unneeded large framework.

A future `HotelScrapeJob` may track:

- source
- destination
- check-in/check-out
- guests/rooms
- status: `queued`, `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `blocked`, `partially_completed`
- timestamps
- result count
- error code/message
- retry count
- freshness/cache key

A job worker should claim queued jobs safely, run one adapter at a time within a source concurrency limit, and update state in transactions. A database uniqueness/claim strategy should prevent duplicate active jobs for the same normalized search conditions and source.

Search freshness should be configurable rather than hardcoded. The proposed default categories are:

- fresh: under 30 minutes
- stale: 30 minutes to 6 hours
- old: over 6 hours

The exact thresholds must be settings/cache-policy values. A stale result should be returned immediately and may trigger a refresh. A failed or blocked refresh must not remove existing observations or break the hotel search response.

## 6. Error, retry, and rate-limit policy

The manager should classify failures into stable outcomes such as:

- `TIMEOUT`
- `NAVIGATION_ERROR`
- `SELECTOR_MISSING`
- `SOURCE_BLOCKED`
- `FORBIDDEN`
- `EMPTY_RESULT`
- `MALFORMED_RECORD`
- `BROWSER_CRASH`
- `VALIDATION_ERROR`

Retry only transient failures such as timeout, temporary navigation failure, or browser crash. Use bounded exponential backoff. Do not repeatedly retry `SOURCE_BLOCKED`, `FORBIDDEN`, CAPTCHA/login challenges, or policy violations. If a source blocks automation, record `SCRAPE_BLOCKED` and continue with other sources; never attempt to circumvent the block.

Every source needs a configurable request delay and concurrency limit. The existing Nominatim one-second delay is not a general source rate-limit mechanism. The Playwright manager should provide per-source limits and should not use proxy rotation, stealth, CAPTCHA solving, or other controls designed to defeat access restrictions.

## 7. Caching strategy

Use the existing Django cache abstraction initially, but define stable normalized keys containing:

- normalized destination/place identity
- check-in and check-out
- guests and rooms
- relevant source/filter values
- adapter/source version where output changes

Example conceptual key:

```text
hotel-search:v1:<place-hash>:<checkin>:<checkout>:<guests>:<rooms>:<source-policy-hash>
```

The search response should be able to report:

- observation age/freshness
- whether a refresh is queued/running
- last successful source collection
- whether a source is blocked or failed

A cache hit must not launch a browser. A cache miss may return database observations first and schedule background collection when the search API is extended. A database-backed freshness index or normalized observation query is required because local-memory cache is process-local and is lost on restart.

## 8. API evolution

Preserve all current endpoints and behavior:

- `GET /api/hotels/`
- `GET /api/hotels/viewport/`
- `POST /api/hotels/search-area/`
- discovery, nearby, geocoding, price, and source endpoints

Only add APIs if needed for the background flow, following the current authenticated DRF conventions. A future search/status API could provide:

- `POST /api/hotels/search/` with destination/dates/guests/rooms and optional filters
- `GET /api/hotels/search-status/<job_id>/`
- `GET /api/hotels/jobs/<id>/` for maintainers, subject to stricter permissions

The initial implementation may use the existing viewport/search-area APIs and a management-command worker, avoiding a new public API until the job contract is settled. Any status endpoint must not reveal stack traces, credentials, internal URLs, or unrestricted scrape controls.

All user-facing destination searches should accept text destinations and keep coordinates internal. Existing viewport/bounding-box behavior remains an internal map operation.

## 9. Security and ethical constraints

The current HTTP client’s SSRF defenses are a useful baseline, but browser navigation needs a separate allowlist. The server must choose configured source adapters; it must never expose a generic `POST /scrape?url=...` surface.

Required controls:

- configured source host/domain allowlist
- HTTPS/HTTP policy per adapter
- no redirects to unallowlisted hosts without revalidation
- timeout and maximum result/page limits
- no authentication bypass, CAPTCHA bypass, paywall bypass, or private API extraction
- no proxy rotation or geographic restriction evasion
- no secrets in logs, raw payload snapshots, or admin pages
- sanitize source-derived text before rendering; escape map popup/card content
- validate source URLs and image URLs if image support is later added
- protect management commands/admin job visibility appropriately
- structured logs with source/job/destination/duration/result count/status, excluding sensitive data
- configurable per-source rate limits and concurrency
- dry-run mode that collects/parses but does not write production observations

Each adapter must document the source’s terms, permitted automation, attribution requirements, and data-use limitations. If permission is unclear, the source remains disabled.

## 10. Observability and administration

A future admin view should show job source, destination, dates, guests, status, start/completion, result count, retry count, and a sanitized error code. Raw page content and secrets should not be shown by default.

Structured logs should use a stable prefix/event and safe fields, for example:

```text
[SCRAPER] source=example job_id=... destination="Sauraha" duration_ms=12400 result_count=18 status=success
```

Metrics should include source success/failure/blocked counts, queue age, job duration, records discovered/normalized/rejected/saved, duplicate rate, and browser restart count. These metrics must not include credentials or personal information.

## 11. Testing strategy

Normal CI must not contact live third-party hotel sites.

### Backend unit tests

- browser manager start/reuse/context/page cleanup using mocked Playwright
- browser crash and timeout recovery
- navigation allowlist and redirect policy
- per-source concurrency and rate limiting
- adapter destination/date/guest/room interaction
- stable semantic/role locator extraction
- price/currency/rating/cancellation/breakfast parsing
- missing price/rating and malformed cards
- blocked/forbidden source classification
- bounded retry behavior
- normalizer and validator status handling
- source metadata and URL validation
- existing hotel deduplication integration
- hotel match/create and observation upsert
- duplicate observations across refreshes
- job lifecycle and duplicate active-job prevention
- freshness classification and cache keys
- API search returning cached/database results without launching Playwright

### Fixtures

Store small deterministic HTML/JSON fixtures under the proposed `data_sources/playwright/fixtures/` directory. They should cover valid results, missing fields, malformed prices, blocked states, and multiple sources. Mock browser pages and network responses rather than using live websites.

### Frontend tests

Using the existing Vitest/testing-library setup, test:

- explicit destination submission
- friendly geocoding labels
- map recenter
- loading/empty/error states
- card and marker selection synchronization
- Search This Area
- freshness/stale indicator
- filter and sorting behavior
- list/map mobile toggle
- condition-aware price display
- accessible labels and keyboard focus

## 12. Deployment and operational constraints

Playwright requires browser binaries and OS libraries. A development setup would include:

```text
python -m pip install playwright
python -m playwright install chromium
```

Production must install Chromium and required system dependencies in the worker image. A normal Django web request must not run a long browser session. Recommended deployment shape:

```text
Django web/frontend: fast API, authentication, database/cache
                         |
                         v
                 job queue/worker service
                         |
                         v
                 Playwright worker process
```

Operational concerns include browser memory, worker concurrency, timeouts, crash restarts, source-specific rate limits, and graceful shutdown. If the deployment platform cannot safely run persistent browser workers, keep the scraper disabled and run it as a controlled worker/management process until suitable infrastructure is available.

## 13. Environment variables (proposed, not currently implemented)

These values should be added to an example environment file, never to the secret `.env` file:

```text
PLAYWRIGHT_ENABLED=false
PLAYWRIGHT_HEADLESS=true
PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSER=chromium
PLAYWRIGHT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
PLAYWRIGHT_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2
SCRAPER_ENABLED=false
SCRAPER_DRY_RUN=false
SCRAPER_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=1800
SCRAPER_MAX_RETRIES=3
PLAYWRIGHT_PROXY_SERVER=
PLAYWRIGHT_PROXY_USERNAME=
PLAYWRIGHT_PROXY_PASSWORD=
```

Proxy values are optional and may be used only for legitimate provider-supported routing/privacy purposes, never for evasion. Source enablement and per-source policy should be configuration-driven as well.

## 14. UX and map integration points

The existing map-first search should remain the primary user experience:

- Replace the current “Destination” presentation with “Where are you going?” and a `Search hotels` primary action.
- Keep dates, guests, and later rooms in the search bar.
- Keep coordinates internal and out of normal inputs/cards; current selected-hotel details should stop displaying raw coordinates in the user-facing card.
- Add quick destinations such as Sauraha, Pokhara, Kathmandu, Bharatpur, and Chitwan without hardcoding a large list.
- On explicit destination submission, geocode, center the map, and load persisted/cached hotels.
- After meaningful map movement, show Search This Area; do not query on every tiny move.
- Add empty-map-click handling: a temporary marker plus “Search hotels here” without exposing coordinates.
- Add a visible recenter control. Browser geolocation must be explicitly user initiated.
- Keep selected marker/card synchronization and the existing desktop split/mobile toggle.
- Add user-facing freshness text and condition-aware price labels only when the API provides them.
- Keep technical provenance in Data Sources rather than overwhelming the main search cards.

These are future implementation points; this inspection intentionally does not modify the current components.

## 15. Recommended implementation sequence after approval

1. Add Playwright dependency and browser-manager tests with a disabled default.
2. Add a database-backed job model/migration and admin registration.
3. Add the source registry, exceptions, manager, and dry-run management command with no live source enabled.
4. Add one permitted source adapter only after its policy and selectors are confirmed.
5. Add normalization/validation and persistence through existing hotel models and deduplication.
6. Add freshness/cache keys and a non-blocking refresh endpoint/status contract.
7. Add the source adapter, manager, and API tests using fixtures/mocks.
8. Improve destination/map/card UX incrementally without changing existing hotel routes.
9. Run hotel, dataset, scraper, frontend, compilation, and `git diff --check` regression checks.

## 16. Known limitations of the current project

- No Playwright package or browser manager exists.
- No background job/worker infrastructure exists.
- No durable distributed cache exists by default; Django local-memory cache is process-local.
- Current `HotelPriceObservation` cannot represent every requested booking condition or review metadata without nullable schema extensions.
- Current frontend does not yet expose a generic destination-search endpoint, freshness state, empty-map search action, or condition-aware comparison UI.
- No live source has been selected, configured, or tested as part of this architecture inspection.
- No application code, dependency, environment file, migration, or scraper was changed by this document.